The Core Mental Model (Getting This Right First)
What you're describing has a name in product design: "Guided AI" — the AI understands intent, the app executes deterministically. This is exactly how Copilot, Claude Code, and good voice assistants work under the hood. The AI is a translator between human language and a pre-defined action graph. It never invents new capabilities — it just finds the right path through ones that already exist.
The way to think about it architecturally:
Human language  →  AI understands intent  →  Action Graph  →  Execution
"I read 30 pages"    { type: 'log_activity',   logActivity()   DB write
                       title: 'Reading',        (already exists
                       value: 30, unit:'pages'} in the UI)
The AI's job ends at the middle box. Everything to the right is deterministic, testable, and completely independent of AI. This is the right way to build it and it's what makes TARS not feel like a toy.

On Smart Contextual Buttons in Chat
Yes, absolutely do this — but design it as a system, not case-by-case. The pattern is called "Quick Reply Chips" and every serious chat interface uses them (Google Assistant, Copilot, Claude itself). The key is: chips should be generated by the action result, not by the AI prompt. This keeps them fast and deterministic.
Here's the system design:
typescriptinterface TARSResponse {
  text: string                    // What TARS says
  chips?: QuickReplyChip[]        // Optional action shortcuts
  confirmCard?: PendingAction     // For write confirmations
}

interface QuickReplyChip {
  label: string                   // What it says on the button
  action: TARSAction              // What it does when tapped
  icon?: string                   // Optional icon
}
Every action handler in TARSActionRegistry can optionally return chips alongside its response. These are hardcoded per action type, not AI-generated. Examples:
After "play music":
[Lo-fi] [Jazz] [Classical] [Ambient] [Rain] [Stop Music]
After "show goals":
[Add New Goal] [Goal Breakdown] [View Progress]
After logging an activity:
[Start Focus Session] [Log Another] [View Today]
After "how am I doing?":
[Weekly Review] [View Goals] [Suggest Activity]
When TARS detects topic = goals but no clear intent:
TARS: "What would you like to do with goals?"
[Show All Goals] [Add New Goal] [View Progress] [Goal Breakdown]
This last pattern — topic detection with disambiguation chips — is what you described and it's genuinely the right UX. The user doesn't have to re-type, they just tap. The topic detection ("user is talking about goals") is done by the intent classifier, and when intent is show_goals_menu or ambiguous-goals, it returns a predefined chip set instead of routing to the AI.
Critical rule: Chips must never be AI-generated text. They must always map to a real TARSAction. If an AI generates a chip label that doesn't map to anything executable, tapping it breaks the experience. Hardcode the chip sets per action type.

On the Draggable Avatar / On-Screen Buddy
The draggable pet idea is good and I want to be specific about how to build it so you don't run into the Three.js positioning nightmare.
The key insight is: separate the avatar from the panel. The avatar is a floating element that lives independently on screen. The panel is a separate UI element that gets triggered by the avatar but isn't attached to it. This is how you avoid the "dragging the panel around" UX problem while still letting the avatar go anywhere.
┌────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  App                                           │
│                                                │
│  [Regular UI — Hub, Goals, etc]                │
│                                                │
│                        ┌──────────────────┐   │
│                        │ AssistantPanel   │   │  ← fixed position, 
│                        │ (right drawer or  │   │    doesn't move
│                        │  bottom sheet)    │   │
│                        └──────────────────┘   │
│                                                │
│     [TARS avatar]                              │  ← draggable, 
│     dragged here by user                       │    anywhere on screen
└────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
For the draggable implementation, don't use Three.js for the drag behavior — use a plain div with pointer events for drag, and put the Three.js canvas inside that div for the visual. Three.js inside a draggable div works fine. Three.js handling drag events itself is painful.
typescript// The avatar is a draggable div containing a small Three.js canvas
// Position stored in Zustand: { x: number, y: number }
// Drag via onPointerDown → onPointerMove → onPointerUp on the wrapper div
// The Three.js canvas is just decorative — it renders the monolith/orb
// Click (not drag) → opens AssistantPanel

function TARSAvatar() {
  const [pos, setPos] = useState({ x: window.innerWidth - 80, y: window.innerHeight - 100 })
  const dragging = useRef(false)
  const offset = useRef({ x: 0, y: 0 })
  
  return (
    <div
      style={{ position: 'fixed', left: pos.x, top: pos.y, width: 56, height: 80, 
               cursor: dragging.current ? 'grabbing' : 'grab', zIndex: 9999 }}
      onPointerDown={(e) => { dragging.current = true; offset.current = { x: e.clientX - pos.x, y: e.clientY - pos.y } }}
      onPointerMove={(e) => { if (dragging.current) setPos({ x: e.clientX - offset.current.x, y: e.clientY - offset.current.y }) }}
      onPointerUp={() => { dragging.current = false }}
      onClick={() => { if (!dragging.current) togglePanel() }}
    >
      <TARSThreeCanvas />  {/* Three.js monolith/orb — purely visual */}
    </div>
  )
}
Save position to localStorage so it remembers where the user put it across refreshes. That's it. No libraries needed.
On voice activation (future scope): The foundation for "always listening" is the Web Speech API's SpeechRecognition with continuous: true and a wake word ("Hey TARS", "Polaris"). This is entirely browser-native, no server needed. It's a legitimate Phase 3 feature and the current button-based voice input is a clean stepping stone to it.

What's Missing — Thinking from a Copilot/Claude Code POV
You asked me to think about what basic utilities a capable assistant needs. This is the right question because Copilot and Claude Code have patterns that took years to discover. Here's what they all share that TARS doesn't have yet:
1. Undo / Rollback
Every write action TARS takes should be undoable for 30 seconds. Claude Code has this implicitly (show diff, user reviews). Copilot has it via standard IDE undo. TARS needs it explicitly because users will confirm things too fast.
Implementation: after a successful write, show a toast with an undo button for 30 seconds. Undo calls DELETE /api/activities/:id or sets the soft-delete flag. This requires storing lastAction: { type, id, timestamp } in the Zustand store. One extra state field, massive trust improvement.
2. TARS State Persistence Across Refreshes
Right now conversation history is session-only (cleared on refresh). This is fine for now but the avatar position, the last few conversation turns, and the panel open/close state should persist across refreshes. Use localStorage with a 24-hour TTL. Nobody wants to re-explain what they were doing after an accidental refresh.
3. An Explicit "What Can I Do?" Capability Discovery Surface
Every good assistant has this. Claude has /help. Copilot has the suggestion list. TARS should have a help command that renders the command reference card as formatted chips in the conversation — not a text wall, but actual tappable buttons grouped by category:
TARS: "Here's what I can do:"

[Navigation ▼]    [Audio ▼]    [Log Activity]
[View Goals]      [Focus Session]    [Weekly Review]
[Ask me anything]
Tapping a category expands to show specific commands as chips. This is the single best onboarding and discoverability tool you can add.
4. Error Recovery UX
When a TARS action fails (network error, validation error, Gemini timeout), the current behavior is undefined. Claude Code shows the error inline and offers retry. Copilot shows a "something went wrong" with a retry button. TARS needs:

Failed actions show a specific error message in the conversation ("Couldn't log that activity — the goal ID wasn't found")
A retry button on the failed message
If the error is AI-side (Gemini timeout), offer to try again OR fall back to manual form

This is the difference between a tool that feels brittle and one that feels solid.
5. Input History (Up Arrow)
This is so small but so impactful. When typing in the AssistantInput, pressing the up arrow should cycle through previous inputs (like a terminal). This is one useState<string[]> for history and 5 lines of onKeyDown logic. Users who use TARS frequently will use the same phrases often ("log 30 pages", "how am I doing"). This saves them re-typing.
6. TARS Should "Know" What Screen You're On
Currently TARS gives the same responses regardless of where the user is in the app. But if the user is on the Goals screen and says "add one", TARS should understand that means "add a goal" not "add an activity". Current screen context should be in the request:
typescript// Add to /api/ai/chat payload
{ 
  message: "add one",
  currentScreen: "Goals",   // ← frontend sends this
  history: [...],
  pendingAction: null
}
The intent classifier gets this as a hint. "Add one" on Goals screen → create_goal. "Add one" on Hub → ambiguous, ask. This is one field, massive UX improvement for short contextual commands.
7. Typing Indicators
When TARS is processing, show the classic three-dot typing animation in the conversation. This is standard chat UX and its absence makes the interface feel broken during the 1-3 second API call. One isTarsTyping boolean in the store, one animated component. Copilot, Claude, and every chat interface has this.
8. Message Timestamps + Collapsible History
For longer conversations, older messages should collapse or show timestamps on hover. Not critical but Claude Code does this well — it doesn't hide history, it just manages visual density. For TARS, the first 6 messages stay visible, older ones are behind a "Show earlier" disclosure. Keeps the panel clean.

On the AI ↔ Tool Interaction Logic
You asked about how AI should properly interact with the tool for something like "add a new activity." This is worth being very precise about because getting it wrong creates a bad experience.
The full flow for a write operation should be:
Step 1 — AI extracts a structured draft:
User: "I just ran 5km this morning"
AI receives: message + PolarisContext (which includes active goals with IDs)
AI returns tool call: {
  name: "log_activity",
  args: {
    title: "Morning Run",
    activityType: "quantity",
    value: 5,
    unit: "km",
    goalId: "goal_xyz",        // AI matched "running" to the Fitness goal from context
    date: "2026-04-15",
    status: "completed"
  }
}
Step 2 — Frontend shows ToolConfirmCard with all fields editable:
┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
│  Log Activity                       │
│  ─────────────────────────────────  │
│  Title:  [Morning Run          ]    │  ← editable
│  Type:   [Quantity        ▼]        │  ← editable
│  Value:  [5    ] [km      ▼]        │  ← editable
│  Goal:   [Fitness Goal    ▼]        │  ← editable, dropdown of goals
│  Date:   [Today           ]         │  ← editable
│                                     │
│  [Accept ✓]  [Edit]  [Cancel ✗]     │
│  Auto-accepting in 5s ●●●○○         │
└─────────────────────────────────────┘
Step 3 — User accepts (or edits and then accepts):
Frontend takes the final params (possibly user-edited) and calls logActivity(params) — the same function the LogActivityForm calls. Not a new TARS-specific endpoint. The same function.
Step 4 — Success → TARS confirms:
TARS: "Done. Morning Run logged — 5km. Fitness goal is now 34% complete."
[Log Another] [Start Focus] [View Today]
The critical thing here is Step 2 must show real dropdowns, not just text. The goal selector should be a dropdown of actual goals (loaded from the store). The unit selector should be a dropdown. The date should be a date picker. The user should be able to correct anything the AI got wrong without re-prompting. This is what separates a good AI interface from a frustrating one — the AI gets 80% right, the user fixes the 20%, and it feels collaborative not adversarial.

The Final Thing Worth Saying
You're building something that sits in a category between a chatbot and an automation tool, and the temptation will be to make TARS smarter over time by giving it more AI autonomy. Resist this. The apps you referenced — Claude Code, Copilot — derive their power from the opposite direction: they're extremely reliable at a well-defined set of things, and they make it easy to correct them when they're wrong.
The best version of TARS is one where every capability is so solid and every correction path so obvious that users trust it immediately. That trust comes from predictability, not capability breadth. Build the registry, build the confirm cards with editable fields, build the undo toast, build the chips — and TARS will feel more capable than a system with twice the AI capability but no correction UX.